{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Restore the staged branch_ledger database on node1 with the original ledger IDs and amounts.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The preserved export defines rows 1001=12.50 and 1002=-3.25, and node1 directly shows those restored rows. Later checks show them retained alongside the two bounded replication-test inserts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c4912fae8bd248ad","cmd-f264368c0227490a","cmd-56fe1e09984a436e"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Establish node2 as an application-readable, read-only replica while node1 remains the sole application writer.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 successfully served application-account reads, rejected an attempted insert, and reported global read_only=1. Node1 reported read_only=0. These states and application reads persisted after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-658677537965490d","cmd-9b082bf94cf34fd6","cmd-8bcb546409c44221","cmd-0cee9758d7ff4cea"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Replicate committed node1 inserts to node2 and catch up after a short disconnect without lost or duplicated ledger rows.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A complete bounded transition was observed: node2 was healthy, replication was stopped with two rows present, node1 committed IDs 1003 and 1004, node2 remained at two rows while disconnected, and after restart of replication it reached four exact rows matching node1 with both threads running and zero lag. Exact row counts, values, and sums demonstrate no loss or duplication.","evidence_ids":["cmd-907be72a1cfd41dc","cmd-3968eb961bd2436f","cmd-580514f3b7b64d59","cmd-21c11665288c4664","cmd-173d24b2fb884ad2","cmd-56fe1e09984a436e"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Use a replication-only account.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The ledger_repl account is restricted to node2's address, has replication privilege, and lacks SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE privileges. Replica status directly identifies that account as the active replication user.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f264368c0227490a","cmd-907be72a1cfd41dc"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Preserve the staged export in /srv/legacy2/mysql-replication-catchup.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The export remained at the required path with the original SHA-256 hash on both nodes, including after restarts.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-78babe0dc1454c95","cmd-30323f54c8cd4f5b","cmd-7d5d60654a824c33"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use legacy-platform-compatible database software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8 and used the RHEL repository's MariaDB 10.5.29 package. Fixed lifecycle observations independently confirm the unchanged platform after execution.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1c5a35e10caa41fb","cmd-cd84fee2bcdb41eb","cmd-30323f54c8cd4f5b","cmd-7d5d60654a824c33","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.93,"summary":"State-changing actions were causally connected to installation, replication configuration, access control, bounded catch-up testing, persistence testing, or cleanup. The one unnecessary/broader-than-needed mutation was opening the generic MySQL firewall service on node2, retained after execution; this exposes port 3306 to all firewall-reachable sources rather than matching the application account's 10.100.28.% scope. Authentication and SELECT-only grants reduce the risk, but a subnet/source-scoped rule would have been tighter. No other unnecessary mutation is evident: the temporary failed-write output was removed, package health remained clean, and neither node had failed services.","evidence_ids":["cmd-658677537965490d","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct transition and final-state evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed well on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart baseline, an acknowledged scheduled restart, a changed boot identity, and recovered MariaDB, data, role, and replication state. Fixed after-prepare and after-executor boot identities independently corroborate both restarts.","limitations":["Lifecycle snapshots independently confirm boot changes, unchanged platforms, healthy package state, no failed services, and MariaDB listeners, but they do not independently query database contents; recovered service, data, and replication state therefore relies on the captured executor commands.","Secrets are appropriately redacted, so credential values cannot be inspected directly; successful authenticated replication and privilege queries provide behavioral evidence instead."]}
